{% markdown %}

## What does it cost to keep?

**Less than redrawing by hand, but not free.** The plugin and its tests come to about 77,000 lines of code, over 40% of it tests and audits, and someone has to keep that working as the product changes.

**So I chose a smaller ongoing commitment.** Brand settings sync automatically, because they are cheap to keep current. Full rebuilds of the library wait for major versions and new components. With rebuilds that infrequent, a paid tool such as [story.to.design](https://story.to.design/docs/extend-stories) didn't make sense for us.

**It only holds while Figma stays downstream.** Copy, presets and visibility in a designer's own file belong to them. Edits to the shared library itself would turn Figma back into a second story. That needs a policy before the next rebuild, and there isn't one yet.

## Why did this work?

**The truth was defined before I started the plugin.** If the schemas and tokens had been vague, the plugin would have had nothing to triangulate from, and the agents' output nothing to be checked against. Syndication had already forced that work, for reasons unrelated to Figma.

**Each discipline gets the same story in its own tool.** Designers get Figma and developers get Storybook, both drawing on the same content rules and brand settings. It's the same move I made in [putting analytics into editorial workflows](/work/2026/impact-story-undrr-analytics-overlay-mcp/): meet each team in the tool it already works in.

